import { JsonReaderCopy } from "../model"

pub fn copy -> JsonReaderCopy = JsonReaderCopy {
  summary: "JsonEncode supplies type-specific conversion from a value to JSON. Add deriving JsonEncode to Settings to build JSON from its fields, then write text with encodeString.",
  taskTitle: "Supply JSON conversion from the settings declaration",
  purpose: "Write application Settings as JSON containing a name and retry count. Specify the fields in the Settings declaration, then display two settings values as JSON text.",
  typescriptBefore: "TypeScript writeSettings constructs an object with the chosen name and retries fields in order, then turns it into text with JSON.stringify. JSON.stringify also handles the newline and quotes in the second name. This example writes values already constructed inside the application.",
  benefit: "deriving JsonEncode supplies JSON conversion from the Settings declaration. Instead of repeating the output field list in a separate function, reuse it as this type's conversion rule. encodeString uses that rule wherever text is needed.",
  setup: "The JsonEncode on this page is a trait in std/prelude. Here, a trait is a common agreement that each supported type supplies a way to turn that type into JSON. struct Settings defines a named type, and deriving JsonEncode supplies conversion following that agreement. This example uses JsonEncode without importing it.",
  after: [
    "name: String and retries: Int give the field names and types. Settings { name: \"Mio\", retries: 2 } constructs a value of that type. String and Int have JSON conversion support, which can be used to convert the complete Settings.",
    "import * as json from \"std/json\" gives the module the alias json. json.encodeString (Settings { ... }) calls a function that produces JSON text from settings. Arguments follow the function name after spaces, and parentheses group inner expressions and calls.",
    "Both outputs place name before retries. The second name's newline and quotes are escaped so they can be read correctly as a JSON string. In the source, \\n represents a newline and \\\" a double quote. They appear as JSON escapes in the output, so the name's newline does not create a line break in the displayed JSON.",
    "pub effect fn main is the output entry point. -> Unit means no useful return value; with Console fails ConsoleError declares console use and possible display failure. do runs both println calls in order; the first _ <- discards a print result before continuing. JSON conversion itself does not use the console.",
  ],
  details: [
    "JsonEncode<A> means type A can be converted to JSON. For example, JsonEncode<Settings> means conversion support exists for Settings. The declaration heading displays trait JsonEncode; A here is the input type. Functions with where JsonEncode<A> require this same support.",
    "This trait provides encodeJson, which converts A to Json. Json represents a JSON value, not yet text. The example's encodeString combines this conversion with writing the Json as a string.",
    "Derivation for this named Settings writes fields in their declaration order. Every field type needs JsonEncode support too. Deriving only JsonDecode does not enable writing; select JsonDecode as well when you also need to read JSON.",
    "The input is assumed to be Settings already. Validating incoming text or business rules such as a nonempty name belongs in separate checking code. The matching TypeScript output here concerns the shown strings and small integers.",
  ],
